<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cariboo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cariboo is a region of the interior plateau of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, between the Coast and Cariboo mountains. Gold was discovered on its creeks in the early 1860s, and the Cariboo Gold Rush made Barkerville its booming centre and led to the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cariboo-wagon-road">Cariboo Wagon Road</a>. The region now lives mainly on ranching, forestry and tourism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cariboo.jpg" alt="Cariboo" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skookum1 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_BC-NW_USA-relief_CaribooplusChilcotin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Cariboo is an intermontane region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, centred on a plateau that stretches from the Fraser Canyon to the Cariboo Mountains. Its name refers to the caribou that were once abundant there, and Governor Douglas used it for the area in dispatches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> as early as 1861. The grasslands of the Cariboo are home to the regionally endangered American badger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Cariboo was the first part of the interior north of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraser-river">Fraser River</a> and its canyon to be settled by non-indigenous people, and it played an important part in the early history of the colony and province. Its boundaries in the historical sense are debatable. At first the name meant the region north of the forks of the Quesnel River, between that river's mouth on the Fraser at Quesnel and the northern end of the Cariboo Mountains, mostly in the Quesnel Highland, where several gold-bearing creeks rise near the head of the Willow River.</p><p>The richest of those creeks, Williams Creek, is the site of Barkerville, which was the capital of the gold rush and the seat of government officials for decades afterwards. As settlement spread south along the route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cariboo-wagon-road">Cariboo Road</a>, over the rolling plateaus and benchlands of the Cariboo Plateau and the lands along the Fraser and Thompson rivers, the name came to cover a much wider area than the goldfields alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The goldfields, once the most settled and most significant part of interior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, are thinly populated, and Barkerville is a museum town. The region's towns include Quesnel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> and Likely in the north, Williams Lake, Horsefly and 150 Mile House in the centre, and 100 Mile House, Lone Butte and Clinton in the south.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cariboo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Cariboo</b> gold rush from 1862 led to the building of the Cariboo Wagon Road.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1858-1866">Colony of British Columbia (1858-1866)</a></li><li><blockquote>Its economy was depressed as <b>Cariboo</b> gold declined, and its population of settlers was small next to the First Nations majority.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1866-1871">Colony of British Columbia (1866-1871)</a></li></ul></section>
